1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
A few drops of blood.

2
00:00:01,667 --> 00:00:05,033
That was the promise behind a $9 billion company.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:11,800
In 2003, Elizabeth Holmes founded Theranos at 19, then dropped out of Stanford.

4
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:17,533
The pitch: a full set of blood tests from one finger prick, on its Edison machine.

5
00:00:17,933 --> 00:00:22,367
By 2014, it was worth $9 billion on paper.

6
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:25,567
Two former secretaries of state sat on its board.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:28,767
Walgreens offered its tests in about 40 stores.

8
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,733
But most tests ran on other companies' machines, not the Edison.

9
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:37,067
One whistleblower was Tyler Shultz, a board member's grandson.

10
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:41,033
In October 2015, the Wall Street Journal exposed it.

11
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:44,367
Theranos voided two years of Edison results.

12
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:45,833
Walgreens walked away.

13
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,267
In 2018, it shut down.

14
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:53,300
In January 2022, Holmes was found guilty of defrauding investors.

15
00:00:53,533 --> 00:00:58,033
Her sentence: 11 years and 3 months, later cut by one year.

16
00:00:58,467 --> 00:01:01,700
To be fair, she testified that she believed in the technology.

17
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:04,533
And she was cleared of defrauding patients.

18
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:07,933
The lesson: famous names are not proof.

19
00:01:08,367 --> 00:01:13,867
At its peak, Theranos had published no peer-reviewed data for outside scientists to check.
